    },
    {
      "kind": "chat",
      "seq": 5,
      "id": "msg_seedA05",
      "sessionId": "ses_seedAmaraHeadache",
      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedAmara"
      },
      "content": "That's a really useful clue. Caffeine withdrawal headaches tend to start a day or so after cutting back and can hang around for several days.[1] They're usually dull and on both sides, like you're describing.[2] Taper rather than stopping cold, keep hydrated, and an over-the-counter painkiller is reasonable if it's safe for you.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-29T21:14:10.539Z",
      "emotion": "happy",
      "emotionSource": "llm",
      "usage": {
        "tokensIn": 2288,
        "tokensCached": 1777,
        "tokensOut": 83,
        "costUsd": 0.000132,
        "energySpent": 2,
        "firstTokenMs": 1440,
        "totalMs": 3720
      },
      "citations": [
        {
          "n": 1,
          "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara2",
          "title": "ED triage guidelines.pdf",
          "type": "file",
          "chunkId": "kch_amaraTri03",
          "locator": "§ 2.4",
          "quote": "Caffeine withdrawal headache usually begins 12 to 24 hours after a sharp cut in intake and can last two to nine days. Gradual tapering, fluids and simple painkillers are usually enough; it rarely needs emergency care.",
          "score": 0.83
        },
        {
          "n": 2,
          "sourceId": "kno_seedAmara2",
          "title": "ED triage guidelines.pdf",
          "type": "file",
          "chunkId": "kch_amaraTri02",
          "locator": "§ 2.1",
          "quote": "Most headaches seen in the department are primary headaches: tension-type, migraine, or medication- and caffeine-related. A dull, pressing pain on both sides without neurological signs is typical of tension-type or withdrawal headache.",
          "score": 0.9
        }
      ],
